#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Keep an OrgSynDB site built from Git, for the container image.

``orgsyndb-deploy run`` repeats a cycle until it is stopped: fetch the
application and the collection from their Git repositories and the
registry snapshot from the lab application, build the site when the
collection commit, the registry or the application commit differs from
the latest import the application accepted, send the application the
build's manifest, and publish the build only once the application has
accepted it. ``orgsyndb-deploy sync`` runs one cycle. ``orgsyndb-deploy
status`` prints the saved state; ``orgsyndb-deploy retry`` forgets the
failed builds, so that the next cycle tries them again. SIGHUP, or a
wake-up (``POST /wake``, below), starts the next cycle of ``run`` at
once, or one more after the cycle that runs.

Settings come from the environment:

ORGSYNDB_COLLECTION_REPO   Git URL or path of the collection (required)
ORGSYNDB_COLLECTION_REF    branch or tag to build (default: main)
ORGSYNDB_COLLECTION_PATH   collection directory inside that repository
                           (default: the repository root)
ORGSYNDB_APP_UPDATE        true to follow the application's repository
                           (default: true)
ORGSYNDB_APP_REPO          Git URL or path of the application (default:
                           the repository the image was built from)
ORGSYNDB_APP_REF           branch or tag of the application (default:
                           the one the image was built from)
ORGSYNDB_INTERVAL          seconds between cycles (default: 300)
ORGSYNDB_PDF               true to render procedure PDFs (default: false)
ORGSYNDB_KEEP_RELEASES     published sites kept besides the current one
                           (default: 2)
ORGSYNDB_BUILD_TIMEOUT     seconds a build, and then its manifest, may
                           take each (default: 3600)
ORGSYNDB_REGISTRY_URL      address of the lab application's registry
                           snapshot (default:
                           http://app:8000/lab/registry/snapshot.json)
ORGSYNDB_MANIFEST_URL      address the manifest of each build is posted
                           to (default:
                           http://app:8000/lab/collection/manifest/)
ORGSYNDB_BUILDER_TOKEN     the token the application expects on a
                           manifest, and one the server takes on a
                           wake-up; without it nothing is built
ORGSYNDB_ALLOWED_HOSTS     the host names the lab application accepts,
                           separated by commas; the first that is not *,
                           without a leading dot, is sent as the Host of
                           the snapshot's, the manifest's and the
                           check's requests (default: localhost)
ORGSYNDB_GATE_TOKEN        the token the gate's hook signs its checks
                           and the push log's hook its wake-ups with;
                           without it every check answers 503
ORGSYNDB_GATE_PORT         the server's port (default: 8091)
ORGSYNDB_GATE_BIND         the address the server listens on
                           (default: 0.0.0.0)
ORGSYNDB_GATE_TIMEOUT      seconds the hook waits for a check; a check
                           ends 10 seconds sooner (default: 150, at
                           least 11)
ORGSYNDB_CHECK_URL         address the collection half of a check is
                           posted to (default:
                           http://app:8000/lab/collection/check/)
ORGSYNDB_PUSH_LOG          the push log Forgejo appends to, read for the
                           pusher of each build (default:
                           /var/lib/orgsyndb-pushes/pushes.log)

The data directory (ORGSYNDB_DATA, default /var/lib/orgsyndb) holds the
collection's clone in ``collection/``, the snapshot last fetched in
``registry.json``, the releases in ``releases/``, the symlink
``current`` to the release being served, and ``state.json``. Every run of
``tools/orgsyndb`` has ORGSYNDB_REGISTRY naming ``registry.json``. A
cycle whose snapshot cannot be fetched builds nothing. The snapshot's
``import`` names the latest accepted import; ``current`` is pointed at
its release whenever that release exists. The application's clone is
ORGSYNDB_APP (default /opt/orgsyndb/app). ORGSYNDB_IMAGE_INFO (default
/opt/orgsyndb/image.json) names the image's record of the repository,
ref and ``deploy/docker`` tree it was built from. An application commit
whose ``deploy/docker`` tree differs needs a new image, and is not used.

Every manifest names as ``pusher`` the user name of the push log's last
line naming the collection commit, ``<time> <commit> <id> <name>``, read
after the build's commands have run; it is null when no line names the
commit, the line's name is ``-`` or the log is missing. A line that is
not of that form is skipped.

With ORGSYNDB_GATE_TOKEN or ORGSYNDB_BUILDER_TOKEN set, ``run`` also
runs the builder's server, from a thread beside its cycles. It answers
``POST /wake`` with 202 and an empty body when the request carries
``Authorization: Bearer`` with either token, and 401 otherwise; a
wake-up does what SIGHUP does.

The server answers ``POST /check`` for the gate, the pre-receive hook
of the collection's repository in Forgejo, and answers it 503 while
ORGSYNDB_GATE_TOKEN is empty. The request carries a commit's
pack, as ``git pack-objects --stdout --revs`` writes it, the commit's id
in ``X-Orgsyndb-Commit`` and ``Authorization: Bearer
<ORGSYNDB_GATE_TOKEN>``. The check checks the commit out as a build
would, validates it and builds its manifest with ``tools/orgsyndb``
against a snapshot of its own, and posts the manifest's collection half
to ORGSYNDB_CHECK_URL with ORGSYNDB_BUILDER_TOKEN. It answers 200 with
an empty body when the collection passes, and otherwise with a status
and the reasons, one per line, in plain UTF-8 text: 422 with the errors
of the commit, 503 when the gate is busy, the check took too long or a
service failed, and 400, 401, 404, 405, 411 or 413 for a request that is
not a check. One check runs at a time; a request while one runs is
answered 503 at once. A commit whose tree holds a symbolic link is
refused before it is checked out. A check uses its own clone of the
application, ``check/app/`` in the data directory, at the application
commit of the published release, or of the cycle's clone when none is
published, with copies of the cycle clone's tool caches, and a directory
of its own under ``check/``, removed when it ends. Everything a check
does, the transfers included, ends by one deadline,
ORGSYNDB_GATE_TIMEOUT less 10 seconds after it starts. Its steps run in
process groups of their own, which are killed at its deadline and when
SIGTERM stops ``run``.
"""

